      "id": "80099b02-3377-4220-b370-ac5a1193f5c0",
      "unit_id": "c59b978a-bc81-465c-95b6-dfaf6f4ef9d2",
      "type": "reading",
      "title": "Reading Practise",
      "content": "<p>Reading is one of the most effective ways to improve language skills. People who read regularly often develop a wider vocabulary and better understanding of grammar. Reading also helps learners become familiar with different writing styles and expressions used by native speakers.</p><p>Many experts recommend reading for at least twenty minutes each day. This habit can improve concentration and critical thinking skills. In addition, reading exposes learners to new ideas, cultures, and perspectives that they may not encounter in their daily lives.</p><p>Technology has made reading more accessible than ever before. Today, people can access books, articles, and educational materials from their phones, tablets, or computers. However, despite these advantages, many individuals still struggle to maintain a consistent reading habit.</p><p>One reason is that social media and other forms of digital entertainment compete for people's attention. As a result, many learners spend less time reading than they intend to. Creating a reading schedule and setting realistic goals can help build a sustainable habit.</p><p>Research has shown that individuals who read frequently are more likely to perform well academically and professionally. Reading not only improves language ability but also strengthens memory and analytical thinking. For these reasons, educators continue to encourage students to make reading a regular part of their daily routine.</p>",
